  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 246 PM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nBy Tuesday night, the surface winds become more northerly instead of \nnortheasterly, which helps to reinforce slightly drier low-level air \ninto the area. This may work to scatter out any low-level stratus \nthat is still lingering in the area, and will also allow \ntemperatures to fall a bit more, especially where clearing happens. \nLows on Wednesday morning are forecast to be in the 50s across the \nentire area providing the first widespread chill of the season. \nAlthough the anomalously strong surface high is located well to the \nnortheast of the area, its slow moving nature maintains northerly to \neasterly low-level flow across the area into the weekend. The result \nfor us will be a gradual warmup through the week, as clearing trends \ncontinue, to temperatures that are near average for late September \nalong with dry conditions. The latest LREF temperature IQRs show \nspreads of 3-5 degrees this week, illustrating higher confidence in \nthese values. By the weekend, there are indications from long-range \nguidance that southerly flow returns to the region as a mid/upper- \nlevel trough traverses across the western United States. A setup \nlike this would lead to southerly flow that would promote the return \nof slightly warmer conditions and increasing chances for rain across \nthe region.",
